## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP with an extra resource dimension (shields) on a classic grid path. Demonstrates handling of constrained resources, 3D DP states, and reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- DP with an additional dimension for resources (k)
- Transition with/without consuming a shield
- Path reconstruction via parent pointers

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxGridPath(grid, K) {
  const n = grid.length, m = grid[0].length;
  const NEG = -Infinity;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array.from({length: m}, () => Array(K+1).fill(NEG)));
  dp[0][0][0] = grid[0][0];
  for (let i=0; i<n; i++) {
    for (let j=0; j<m; j++) {
      for (let k=0; k<=K; k++) {
        const v = grid[i][j];
        if (i>0) dp[i][j][k] = Math.max(dp[i][j][k], dp[i-1][j][k] + v);
        if (j>0) dp[i][j][k] = Math.max(dp[i][j][k], dp[i][j-1][k] + v);
        if (k>0 && v < 0) {
          if (i>0) dp[i][j][k] = Math.max(dp[i][j][k], dp[i-1][j][k-1]);
          if (j>0) dp[i][j][k] = Math.max(dp[i][j][k], dp[i][j-1][k-1]);
        }
      }
    }
  }
  return Math.max(...dp[n-1][m-1]);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you recover one optimal path and its moves?
- How to optimize space to O(n*m*K)?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP with adjacency constraint and exact count; requires states over i and j, base cases, and backtracking.

## Key Concepts
- DP with two dimensions n and k
- adjacency constraint via i-2 transition
- reconstruction via parent pointers or decisions
- space/time bounds and potential optimizations

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxSumNoAdj(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const K = Math.min(k, Math.floor((n + 1) / 2));
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(K + 1).fill(0));
  const take = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(K + 1).fill(false));
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j <= K; j++) {
      dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j];
      if (j > 0) {
        if (i >= 2) {
          const cand = dp[i - 2][j - 1] + A[i - 1];
          if (cand > dp[i][j]) {
            dp[i][j] = cand;
            take[i][j] = true;
          }
        } else if (i === 1 && j === 1) {
          if (A[0] > dp[i][j]) { dp[i][j] = A[0]; take[i][j] = true; }
        }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruct
  let i = n, j = K, indices = [];
  while (i >= 1 && j >= 0) {
    if (take[i][j]) { indices.push(i - 1); i -= 2; j -= 1; }
    else { i -= 1; }
  }
  indices.reverse();
  return { sum: dp[n][K], indices };
}
```

